# Corpus licence

The Parsimony evaluation corpus in this directory —

- `conversations.jsonl` — 151 conversations across six query classes
- `adversarial_pairs.jsonl` — 66 near-duplicate pairs (45 adversarial, 21 control)
- `gold.jsonl` — 40 questions with human-written reference answers
- `MANIFEST.sha256` — the freeze hashes

— is licensed under **Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
(CC BY 4.0)**.

Full licence text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode
Human-readable summary: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

## What this means

You may share and adapt this corpus for any purpose, including commercially,
provided you give appropriate credit and indicate if changes were made.

## Attribution

> Parsimony evaluation corpus. Arrsh Tripathi, Alok Singh and Dev Singh,
> Vellore Institute of Technology, 2026. CC BY 4.0.

## Provenance

Every conversation, adversarial pair and gold answer in this corpus was written
by the authors. No text was scraped, and no third-party dataset is included or
required. The corpus contains no personal data, real or synthetic-realistic.

The corpus is **frozen**: `MANIFEST.sha256` records the content hashes, and that
hash is written into every row of the experiment ledger. Any post-freeze edit
changes the hash and makes the affected results visibly incomparable, which is
the point — a self-authored corpus is only defensible if it can be shown to
predate the results (see `docs/03-decision-log.md`, ADR-015).
